Z||000007||RossT||02-22-2001||01:06 AM||||Just a couple of ideas.<P>1. I have heard that replacement waterpumps sometimes hace plastic impellars that do not move the fluid very well.  Alway buy one with a metal impellar.<P>2. I have also heard that the coolant tubes can accumulate alot of crap in them to apoint that nothing (or just a little bit of coolant passed thru to make them warm to the touch) goes thru.  People have had to actually take them off and have them flushed out. Just like a clogged radiator.<P>Just a couple of ideas||||reg||1
Z||000008||sqoach||02-22-2001||01:12 AM||||the stat is not even a month old. and i made sure it worked. the water pump is about 3 or 4 months old. and it does have the metal impellars. made sure of that. i considered taking out all of the pipes and the radiator, but didn't. i guess that may be my next move.<P>thanks for replying guys. i got a little outta hand with my opening post.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P>other suggestions welcome with open arms  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||rodmcneill||02-22-2001||01:24 AM||||Have you checked for collapsing hoses?||||reg||5
Z||000010||RossT||02-22-2001||01:29 AM||||With the stat out, are you getting flow thru the system?  is the return coolant tube getting hot, or is it just warm. Both side should be hot all the way down the tubes, if not you may have a partial obstruction in either tube or the radiator or that water pump may not be pumping!!!!!!!!!!!!!<p>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 02-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||FieroSTETZ||02-22-2001||02:27 AM||||someone let me know if i sound way off, but i once had an inline 4 in my old mazda and it had a similar problem, tried everything. what wound up happening is the head gasket went and when the car started to heat up it would put exhaust gases into the coolant system, and soon after would start overheating. i think that a radiator shop could put a doohicky on your coolant fill cap that would maybe pressurize the system enough to push some coolant out (maybe?) that was a friggin irritating problem- someone let me know if i sound close?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1012904&a=7460757&p=25516673&Sequence=0&res=high"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||My7Fieros||02-22-2001||03:02 AM||||Check:<BR>Radiator..could be clogged<P>Coolant lines..may be clogged (or collapsed)<P>And one more thing about the fan...<BR><B>MAKE SURE THE FAN IS SPINNING IN THE RIGHT DIRECTION!!!!</B> May sound strange, but it does happen...especially when the fan or relay gets replaced. I actually learned this here........<BR>When the fan kicks on, hold a paper towel in front of it...<B>it should blow the paper towl..if the towl is going towards the fan, like its getting sucked in, then your fans is turning in the wrong direction and that will make a substancial difference! </B><BR>....has something to do with the two wires being connected the wrong way..like I said, I read it on here a while back...just a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Carrolles||02-22-2001||07:14 AM||||Did you recently replace the radiator cap and if so is it a Stant one?<P>Autozone and other stores list a Vented cap for Fieros.  Fieros use a Non-Vented cap.<P>In Fieros, Vented caps let coolant dribble out the overflow bottle when the system is shut down.<P>This happened to my 85GT.  As little as a cup of water can cause an air bubble to air lock the water pump momentarily and cause the skyrocketing temps.  It happened to me.<P>A proper cap and burping fixed my problem.<P>Hope this helps.  Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000014||SteveJ||02-22-2001||08:39 AM||||Sounds like you just replaced a bunch of cooling parts recently. If it didn't overheat before that then it is one of those parts.<BR>Sounds like you are saying the temperature just spikes up all at once and not too long after the engine starts. I have to suspect the new thermostat is not opening. Open it up pull out the thermostat put it on the bench and close the system back up again. Start her up. If it doesn't overheat take that thermostat back to the parts counter and politely ask for a replacement.<BR>If it still overheats, like previously mentioned, check the tubes to and from the radiator. If either is cold you have a plugged radiator. You are lucky its winter so you can tell if the pipes are warming. <BR>Did you run some kind of radiator flush in this system. Did you loosen all the crap which then flowed around in the system until it found the smallest opening which it couldn’t get through which is your radiator and heater? <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||lou_dias||02-22-2001||09:12 AM||||Maybe you're just running too damn lean...||||reg||1
Z||000016||SteveJ||02-22-2001||09:26 AM||||No no no he's not lean. Go back and read his second post about sixth down. He says the temp shoots right up really fast. - thermastat - no water circulation. <BR>Say sqoach, Tell us why you think its overheated. Is it boiling over? From where? Dash board light going on? . . .and is the light all and then you shut it down (smart)?<BR>Come on more info boy, we need more info.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||SteveJ||02-22-2001||09:34 AM||||I am assuming its in the driveway at idle when this is happening. If not say so.||||reg||1
Z||000018||sqoach||02-22-2001||12:24 PM||||thanks for replying guys. i just woke up about an hour ago, so that's why i'm replying a little late. yes, i'm lazy   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>radiator cap is 16lb non-vented, about a month old. and it's a stant brand. i even have the part # (10230) water pump works cuz when i take off the thermostat cap, i can see the coolant flowing. last i checked, all the lines heated up. (about a month ago i checked that) fan spins like it's suppose to. and i've flushed out the system before. and it doesn't overheat all the time. just after i've driven it 5 or 6 times after i've burped the system. so it does just fine for a while. i can drive around all day and it will do fine.<P>but when it does overheat, it happens without warning. the temp just shoots up (sometimes up then back down) i don't let it get hot enough for the temp light to come on. i shut it down, then check the coolant level up front, and it's risen a lot. i keep the coolant level about halfway when not running. there were a few times when it did overflow because i had the coolant level too high to begin with.<P>i suspect a leak somewhere, and that's getting air into the system. but i can't find coolant spots anywhere in the place i park. i'm also gonna check all the lines and the radiator when i get some time.<p>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 02-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||fropuff||02-22-2001||12:29 PM||||I have the EXACT SAME problem with my GT.  I've burped the system a time or two; had to refill it when all of the coolant spilled on the road.  I have to check the water pump, but all the other stuff is good.  Fan works, thermostat works, no collapsed tubes or lines, lines heat up.  I drive it, thinking it's all good, and the damn thing overheats!  Sucks.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000020||N_Man||02-22-2001||12:54 PM||||Well sqoach based on your last post, my guess is an air bubble trapped in the system.  How it got in there, i dunno.  But it sounds like the air bubble goes past the sensor and the temp shoots up.  I would also be willing to bet that if you didn't shut it down right away your coolant would spill out onto the road.<P>so how sure are you that when you burp it you get ALL of the air out?||||reg||1
Z||000021||ferg||02-22-2001||01:05 PM||||I had a problem similar to this in my chevy p/u. I did all the same symptoms. The problem turned out to be 1) blown head gasket which I had replaced - only to find that I cracked one of the heads. So I got new heads and had a valve job done- problem was solved. you might give that a try. Also- I didn't have any external leaks, nor did I have any coolant in the oil- checked that as well. good luck||||reg||1
Z||000022||sqoach||02-22-2001||01:36 PM||||well, i'm not sure that i got ALL of the air out. but, is it possible to get it all out? i know i get enough of the air out for it to work for a while. but there has to be a leak or something. maybe even the head gasket. i plan on doing a rebuild this spring, or when it warms up for a while. then i can check for that and possibly a cracked head. the engine has 126K+ miles, so it's due for a rebuild. i'm pretty sure the valve seals are shot too and the rings. i guess i'll just have to wait and see.||||reg||1
Z||000023||SteveJ||02-22-2001||01:54 PM||||This isn't rocket science.<BR>you said:<BR>“but when it does overheat, it happens without warning. the temp just shoots up (sometimes up then back down) i don't let it get hot enough for the temp light to come on. i shut it down, then check the coolant level up front, and it's risen a lot. i keep the coolant level about halfway when not running. there were a few times when it did overflow because i had the coolant level too high to begin with."<P>If the temperature "just shoots up" the thermostat is closed. So you have a sticky thermostat in there. Sometimes it works, sometimes it doesn't. Quality control isn't what it should be on those things. "(sometimes up then back down) " - it opened, late. Get it?<BR>. . . and you were sitting there looking at the gauge worrying about it so you noticed it. With a 16 yr old girl in a Dodge Neon nobody would know it was happening or even care. Your looking at the temp gauge. And then you go around and look at the overflow bottle to confirm your suspicions. Mind you nothing evil has happened. No spilled fluid, no steam, no leaks. If your system is expanding fluid into the overflow bottle and then sucking it back in you don't have a leak in the system. If you did you would not suck the fluid back in. You would suck air in from the leak. PseudoAxiom: If it sucks, it's good. <P>I think your thermostat is bad and they are so damn cheap and easy to change go do it. Buy a brand name. Keep the receipt. I saw a fried go through three of 'um once buthtey were cheapies.<P>Try driving it with no thermostat in it. The engine should never heat up properly. That will confirm in your mind that all the cooling is in place and works well. It won't hurt anything of course your mileage will be rotten and performance will drop off.<P>You didn't answer a previous question. Did this problem exist before you tuned up the cooling system?||||reg||1
Z||000024||sqoach||02-22-2001||03:30 PM||||it doesn't suck all the coolant back in though. i checked it just a little while ago, some of it sucked back in, but it's not as low as it was before this happened. i know cuz i keep a close eye on this. and i've tried running it with the stat out. same thing would happen except it ran dead cold. then it would heat up real fast. the reason is hasn't overflowed lately is because i've been watching it carefully. and as soon as it heats up i turn off the car. if i were to let it run a bit longer, then it would overflow.<P>if i do happen to go get another thermostat, what brand should i get? i always get the stant brand, cuz that's what they give me at the parts store.<P>and yes, this problem has been going on for a long time, that's why i did anything to begin with.<p>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 02-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||Carrolles||02-22-2001||03:45 PM||||Since all the fluid is not being drawn back into the radiator from the overflow bottle I'd suspect there is a leak in the bottle diptube or the hose between the bottle and the radiator (maybe leaking where the hose is clamped to the filler tube neck).<P><BR>If there is a small leak in the hose the pressure from the radiator will send the coolant into the overflow bottle but it will suck some air when it draws coolant back into the radiator.  Each time this happens you get a bit more air into the system.<P>I've had this happen on a Bonneville.  The molded plastic bottle was slightly split in the seam on the diptube part.  I used a soldering iron and welded the seam shut.  The bottles are made of Thermoplastic which means you can melt it back together.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000026||SteveJ||02-22-2001||04:20 PM||||Oh, bad news. You ran it with the thermostat out and it overheated - bad. . . and you have burped it well, and it runs cool for long periods before all of a sudden it creates a temperature spike. Hum, time to start looking at the zebras. Sort of sounds like it makes another bubble by itself. Most likely in the head. Makes that previous post of head gasket look better.||||reg||9
Z||000027||Firebug||02-22-2001||05:00 PM||||Have you looked at the timing at all?  If you are running an engine very advanced, it would cause the engine to run hotter.  Also as someone stated before, a lean problem could also cause this problem.  <P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000028||sqoach||02-22-2001||06:04 PM||||i looked at the tube that runs from the overflow to the radiator. it wasn't on there real tight. so i readjusted it and tighted the clamp thingy.<P>also, i was just about to change my oil, cuz it's about due for an oil change, and i noticed something. when i took off the oil cap, there was this milky tan looking stuff around the cap and the opening. i looked at the oil i drained and it looked normal. and i looked down into the valve cover and that oil looked normal too. it was just the stuff on the cap and around the opening that had that color. i know that if your oil has a milky look to it, coolant has gotten in there. but why doesn't all my oil look this color?<p>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 02-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000029||SteveJ||02-22-2001||06:33 PM||||Milky oil reveals water. Could be from the coolant or could be condensation. Condensation is usually from a car that hasn't been run for a while and will boil off as the car runs. There isn't much. <BR>If this car has been run and has been up to temperature there shouldn't be any.<P>You need a pressure test at a service station. They presurize the cooling system and see if it leaks down.||||reg||1
Z||000030||theogre||02-22-2001||07:53 PM||||You replaced the radiator cap. did you replace the thermostat one? That could bleed air in but hold coolant. Check the base of the thermostat house very carefully.<P>run it for awhile w/o the thermostat. The one that's in there could be sticking but not all the time.<P>If the oil was only milky tan by the car replace the cap and the PCV vent gromets. If you were getting coolant in the oil the oil in the pan should have shown something. It's a bit if a hard call but I'd start with the cap and grommets.<P>Unless....<P>I'd do a compression test. You could have a head gasket on it's way out. If it is just starting to go the test has to be done very carefully. You have to make sure each cylinder cranks over the same number of times. (It seems obvious to some of us but that one critical bit gets forgotten allot.)<P>If the gasket is just starting to go it can do this. finding it can be a ____. Even the compression test could have a hard time.<P>One posibility would be to try one of the oil analysis services. You can get the kit for these at many parts stores. I've also seen them in K/Wall mart on occasion. That analysis can tell you if coolant is ending up in the oil even if it's not enough to turn the oil a color. I've never used one of these myself so I can't tell you what service is best or how accurate they are. I just thought it might be something to try.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 02-22-2001).]||||reg||1
